{"subjectId":"sql","id":"sample-database","title":"The Sample Database","group":"Getting Started","order":4,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Every example in these notes runs against this small company-and-shop database. Skim it once — then every query result is easy to verify by eye.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sitemap\"></i>The tables</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Rows</th><th>What it holds</th><th>Links</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>departments</code></td><td>4</td><td>Departments and their city</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>employees</code></td><td>10</td><td>Staff with salary, hire date and manager</td><td><code>dept_id</code> → departments, <code>manager_id</code> → employees (self)</td></tr><tr><td><code>customers</code></td><td>5</td><td>Shop customers</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>products</code></td><td>7</td><td>Catalogue with category and list price</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>orders</code></td><td>8</td><td>One row per order, with status</td><td><code>customer_id</code> → customers</td></tr><tr><td><code>order_items</code></td><td>13</td><td>Lines of each order (quantity × price paid)</td><td><code>order_id</code> → orders, <code>product_id</code> → products</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\">customers</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">orders</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">order_items</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">products</div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Schema (DDL)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — schema</figcaption><pre><code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;\n\nCREATE TABLE departments (\n    dept_id   INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    name      TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    location  TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE employees (\n    emp_id      INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    name        TEXT    NOT NULL,\n    email       TEXT    UNIQUE,\n    dept_id     INTEGER REFERENCES departments(dept_id),\n    manager_id  INTEGER REFERENCES employees(emp_id),\n    salary      INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (salary &gt; 0),\n    hire_date   TEXT    NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE customers (\n    customer_id  INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    name         TEXT NOT NULL,\n    city         TEXT,\n    signup_date  TEXT NOT NULL\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE products (\n    product_id  INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    name        TEXT    NOT NULL,\n    category    TEXT    NOT NULL,\n    price       INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (price &gt;= 0)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n    order_id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    customer_id  INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES customers(customer_id),\n    order_date   TEXT    NOT NULL,\n    status       TEXT    NOT NULL\n                 CHECK (status IN ('pending', 'shipped', 'delivered', 'cancelled'))\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE order_items (\n    order_id    INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES orders(order_id),\n    product_id  INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES products(product_id),\n    quantity    INTEGER NOT NULL CHECK (quantity &gt; 0),\n    unit_price  INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    PRIMARY KEY (order_id, product_id)\n);</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">SQLite</div><p><code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON</code> is SQLite-specific: SQLite enforces foreign keys only when this is switched on for the connection. Other engines always enforce them.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>The data</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT * FROM departments ORDER BY dept_id;\nSELECT * FROM employees ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | name        | location\n--------+-------------+----------\n1       | Engineering | Bengaluru\n2       | Sales       | Mumbai\n3       | HR          | Hyderabad\n4       | Marketing   | Pune\n\nemp_id | name   | email          | dept_id | manager_id | salary | hire_date\n-------+--------+----------------+---------+------------+--------+-----------\n1      | Asha   | asha@corp.in   | 1       | NULL       | 150000 | 2015-03-01\n2      | Ravi   | ravi@corp.in   | 1       | 1          | 120000 | 2017-06-15\n3      | Meera  | meera@corp.in  | 1       | 2          | 95000  | 2019-01-10\n4      | Karan  | karan@corp.in  | 1       | 2          | 95000  | 2020-08-01\n5      | Priya  | priya@corp.in  | 2       | 1          | 110000 | 2016-11-20\n6      | Arjun  | arjun@corp.in  | 2       | 5          | 70000  | 2021-02-14\n7      | Neha   | NULL           | 2       | 5          | 72000  | 2022-05-30\n8      | Vikram | vikram@corp.in | 3       | 1          | 80000  | 2018-09-05\n9      | Sara   | sara@corp.in   | 3       | 8          | 85000  | 2023-01-09\n10     | Dev    | NULL           | NULL    | 1          | 60000  | 2024-04-01</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Built-in quirks worth noticing — later topics rely on them:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Asha</strong> has no manager (<code>manager_id</code> is <code>NULL</code>) — she is the root of the hierarchy.</li><li><strong>Dev</strong> has no department (<code>dept_id</code> is <code>NULL</code>) and <strong>Marketing</strong> has no employees — perfect for outer joins.</li><li><strong>Meera</strong> and <strong>Karan</strong> earn the same (95000) — ties for ranking functions.</li><li><strong>Neha</strong> and <strong>Dev</strong> have no email — <code>NULL</code> handling.</li><li><strong>Sara</strong> (85000) earns more than her manager <strong>Vikram</strong> (80000).</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT * FROM customers ORDER BY customer_id;\nSELECT * FROM products ORDER BY product_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | name   | city      | signup_date\n------------+--------+-----------+------------\n1           | Ananya | Mumbai    | 2023-11-02\n2           | Rahul  | Delhi     | 2023-12-15\n3           | Sneha  | Bengaluru | 2024-01-20\n4           | Imran  | Delhi     | 2024-02-01\n5           | Kavya  | NULL      | 2024-03-10\n\nproduct_id | name       | category    | price\n-----------+------------+-------------+------\n1          | Laptop     | Electronics | 55000\n2          | Headphones | Electronics | 2500\n3          | Desk       | Furniture   | 8000\n4          | Chair      | Furniture   | 4500\n5          | Notebook   | Stationery  | 60\n6          | Pen        | Stationery  | 20\n7          | Monitor    | Electronics | 12000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT * FROM orders ORDER BY order_id;\nSELECT * FROM order_items ORDER BY order_id, product_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>order_id | customer_id | order_date | status\n---------+-------------+------------+----------\n101      | 1           | 2024-01-05 | delivered\n102      | 2           | 2024-01-12 | delivered\n103      | 1           | 2024-02-03 | delivered\n104      | 3           | 2024-02-14 | shipped\n105      | 4           | 2024-02-20 | cancelled\n106      | 2           | 2024-03-01 | delivered\n107      | 3           | 2024-03-08 | pending\n108      | 1           | 2024-03-15 | shipped\n\norder_id | product_id | quantity | unit_price\n---------+------------+----------+-----------\n101      | 1          | 1        | 55000\n101      | 2          | 1        | 2500\n102      | 4          | 2        | 4500\n102      | 6          | 10       | 20\n103      | 5          | 5        | 60\n103      | 6          | 5        | 20\n104      | 3          | 1        | 8000\n104      | 4          | 1        | 4500\n105      | 2          | 2        | 2500\n106      | 1          | 1        | 52000\n107      | 5          | 20       | 60\n108      | 2          | 1        | 2500\n108      | 3          | 1        | 8000</code></pre></figure>\n<ul><li><strong>Kavya</strong> has never ordered and has no city; the <strong>Monitor</strong> has never been sold.</li><li>Order <strong>106</strong> sold a Laptop at 52000 — <code>unit_price</code> records the price <em>actually paid</em>, which can differ from the list price in <code>products</code>.</li><li>Order <strong>105</strong> was cancelled — most revenue queries must exclude it.</li></ul>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-database\"></i>Data insert script</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL — data</figcaption><pre><code>INSERT INTO departments VALUES\n    (1, 'Engineering', 'Bengaluru'),\n    (2, 'Sales',       'Mumbai'),\n    (3, 'HR',          'Hyderabad'),\n    (4, 'Marketing',   'Pune');\n\nINSERT INTO employees VALUES\n    (1,  'Asha',   'asha@corp.in',   1,    NULL, 150000, '2015-03-01'),\n    (2,  'Ravi',   'ravi@corp.in',   1,    1,    120000, '2017-06-15'),\n    (3,  'Meera',  'meera@corp.in',  1,    2,     95000, '2019-01-10'),\n    (4,  'Karan',  'karan@corp.in',  1,    2,     95000, '2020-08-01'),\n    (5,  'Priya',  'priya@corp.in',  2,    1,    110000, '2016-11-20'),\n    (6,  'Arjun',  'arjun@corp.in',  2,    5,     70000, '2021-02-14'),\n    (7,  'Neha',   NULL,             2,    5,     72000, '2022-05-30'),\n    (8,  'Vikram', 'vikram@corp.in', 3,    1,     80000, '2018-09-05'),\n    (9,  'Sara',   'sara@corp.in',   3,    8,     85000, '2023-01-09'),\n    (10, 'Dev',    NULL,             NULL, 1,     60000, '2024-04-01');\n\nINSERT INTO customers VALUES\n    (1, 'Ananya', 'Mumbai',    '2023-11-02'),\n    (2, 'Rahul',  'Delhi',     '2023-12-15'),\n    (3, 'Sneha',  'Bengaluru', '2024-01-20'),\n    (4, 'Imran',  'Delhi',     '2024-02-01'),\n    (5, 'Kavya',  NULL,        '2024-03-10');\n\nINSERT INTO products VALUES\n    (1, 'Laptop',     'Electronics', 55000),\n    (2, 'Headphones', 'Electronics',  2500),\n    (3, 'Desk',       'Furniture',    8000),\n    (4, 'Chair',      'Furniture',    4500),\n    (5, 'Notebook',   'Stationery',     60),\n    (6, 'Pen',        'Stationery',     20),\n    (7, 'Monitor',    'Electronics', 12000);\n\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES\n    (101, 1, '2024-01-05', 'delivered'),\n    (102, 2, '2024-01-12', 'delivered'),\n    (103, 1, '2024-02-03', 'delivered'),\n    (104, 3, '2024-02-14', 'shipped'),\n    (105, 4, '2024-02-20', 'cancelled'),\n    (106, 2, '2024-03-01', 'delivered'),\n    (107, 3, '2024-03-08', 'pending'),\n    (108, 1, '2024-03-15', 'shipped');\n\nINSERT INTO order_items VALUES\n    (101, 1, 1, 55000), (101, 2, 1, 2500),\n    (102, 4, 2, 4500),  (102, 6, 10, 20),\n    (103, 5, 5, 60),    (103, 6, 5, 20),\n    (104, 3, 1, 8000),  (104, 4, 1, 4500),\n    (105, 2, 2, 2500),\n    (106, 1, 1, 52000),\n    (107, 5, 20, 60),\n    (108, 2, 1, 2500),  (108, 3, 1, 8000);</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>SELECT *</code> is fine for exploring. In application code, list the columns you need: it is faster, and the query does not break (or silently change) when someone adds a column.</p></div></section>"}